---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// A condensed, serious version of the application statement:
// bio → SYMBIONT → biofeedback lineage → repair (Ukraine) → "with, not about".
const paras = [
  "I am a composer and sound artist from Kalush, in western Ukraine. I studied in Lviv and work between Hamburg and Gießen. For most of a decade I have made sound for live performance — composing and playing for theatre, dance, film and installation, where the music is never finished in advance but tuned, in real time, to a text fragmenting on stage, a body moving through a dreamscape, a collective rehearsing catastrophe. I began as a guitarist, and the guitarist's discipline still governs everything I do: to listen to what is already sounding in a room, and answer it.",
  "My central body of work, SYMBIONT, carries that discipline into an instrument. It keeps the structure of an improvising duet exactly — and replaces one of the two performers with a living system: an EEG signal, a plant, or the electrical weather of a room, rendered as sound the moment it happens. The apparatus is deliberately modest — consumer sensors, off-the-shelf parts — because the question it asks is not technological but relational: what does a symbiosis sound like when the human ear is only one of several nervous systems in the room?",
  "I place this work in the lineage of biofeedback music — Alvin Lucier, David Rosenboom, Richard Teitelbaum — but where that tradition asked what the body sounds like, I am asking what it means to compose with a living thing rather than about it: to treat authorship as something shared with a system that has its own tempo, its own thresholds, its own refusals.",
  "Before I worked in sound I was a doctoral researcher at the National Academy of Sciences of Ukraine, building the environmental models inside the EU's nuclear-emergency decision-support system — radionuclide transport in rivers, transboundary flood forecasting, catchment work reaching from Chernobyl to Fukushima. For years my material was the data of catastrophe, turned into forecasts. I make sound now with the same instruments turned the other way: I use scientific tools unscientifically, to make a living system audible rather than actionable.",
  "Underneath the technical vocabulary the work is animated by repair. I make it as a Ukrainian, from a home that has itself been shattered and scattered by war; repair is not a metaphor I reach toward but the condition I work in. I build small, listen closely, and let the material — a brainwave, a plant's fluctuation, a room's resonance — decide what the piece can become.",
];
---

<Base title="Oleksandr Mykhalskyi — Statement" active="Statement">
  <article class="statement wrap">
    <header class="st-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Statement</p>
      <h1 class="display distort">With, not&nbsp;about.</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="body">
      {paras.map((p) => <p class="prose">{p}</p>)}
    </div>

    <p class="sig mono">Oleksandr Mykhalskyi · Kalush → Hamburg / Gießen</p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .statement { padding-top: 3rem; padding-bottom: 4rem; }
  .st-head { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .st-head .eyebrow { display: block; margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
  .st-head h1 { font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 1.8rem + 3vw, 4.5rem); }

  .body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.6rem;
    max-width: 42rem;
  }
  .body .prose {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .body .prose:first-child { color: var(--ink); }

  .sig { margin-top: 3.5rem; color: var(--ink-faint); }
</style>
